The Tonga flag geometry produces a matrix whose rows take at most three distinct values (all-\(a\) rows outside the canton; rows that intersect the horizontal arm of the cross; and rows that intersect the canton but miss the horizontal arm). Consequently the row space has dimension at most 3. For generic \(a\neq b\) the three row vectors are linearly independent, attaining rank exactly 3.

This is consistent with the decomposition into at most four rank-1 rectangle indicators plus the all-ones matrix, but the geometry forces those five matrices to be linearly dependent, tightening the bound from 5 to 3. Non-aligned or non-rectangular grids cannot increase the number of distinct rows beyond three while keeping every pixel uniformly colored.

