1.text:
The Tonga flag consists of a uniform red field (value \(a\)) plus a white canton (value \(b\)) containing a centered red cross (value \(a\)). Every row of the resulting matrix therefore belongs to one of three families:

- rows lying entirely outside the canton height (all entries \(a\));
- rows intersecting the canton but missing the horizontal arm of the cross (pattern \(b\ldots b\,a\ldots a\,b\ldots b\) inside the canton block, \(a\) elsewhere);
- rows intersecting the horizontal arm (all entries \(a\) inside the canton width).

These three distinct row vectors span the row space, so \(\operatorname{rank}(M)\le 3\). For generic \(a\neq b\) they are linearly independent, attaining rank exactly 3. The five-term rectangle decomposition mentioned by C2 is linearly dependent once the global support of the all-ones matrix is taken into account; the claimed 6-by-6 counter-example necessarily violates the flag’s rectangular geometry or its uniform per-region coloring. Non-grid-aligned pixelations cannot create additional distinct rows without breaking the uniform-color constraint.

Thus the maximal rank is 3.

